  "worked_solution": "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=3x.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061X.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX={90}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={90}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n \\\\ 3x & ={90}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n \\\\ x & ={30}^{\u2218}+{120}^{\u2218}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by $x={30}^{\u2218}+{120}^{\u2218}\\cdot n,$ where $n$ is an integer.",
